window.AUTH=window.AUTH||{};window.AUTH["22-913"]={"d":"22-913","id":"2023/22-913","n":"Devillier v. Texas","briefs":[{"k":"petitioner","label":"Brief of petitioners Richard Devillier, et al.","date":"2023-11-13","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/22/22-913/289408/20231113141405156_Devillier%20-%20Merits%20Brief_PDFA%20TO%20FILE%2011.13.23.pdf","side":"pet","n":73},{"k":"united-states","label":"Brief amicus curiae of United States","date":"2023-12-12","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/22/22-913/293160/20231212155157672_22-913bsacUnited%20States.pdf","side":"us","n":71},{"k":"respondent","label":"Brief of respondent Texas (DEC. 19, 2023)","date":"2023-12-13","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/22/22-913/293327/20231213180521652_22-913_Brief.pdf","side":"res","n":84},{"k":"reply","label":"Reply of petitioners Richard Devillier, et al.","date":"2024-01-05","url":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/DocketPDF/22/22-913/294991/20240105104347913_Devillier%20-%20Reply%20Brief%20For%20Petitioners.pdf","side":"pet","n":56}],"opinion":"https://www.supremecourt.gov/opinions/23pdf/601us2r15_apl1.pdf","tr":true,"items":[{"t":"case","label":"Agins v. Tiburon","cite":"598 P.2d 25","y":1979,"court":"Cal.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/P.2d/598/25/","by":{"0":"11"}},{"t":"case","label":"Aldrich v. Wainwright","cite":"479 U.S. 918","y":1986,"url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/479/918/","sc":1,"by":{"0":"ii"}},{"t":"case","label":"Azul-Pacifico, Inc. v. City of Los Angeles","cite":"973 F.2d 704","y":1992,"court":"9th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/973/704/","by":{"0":"6, 40","2":"6","3":"6"}},{"t":"case","label":"Backus v. Lebanon","cite":"11 N.H. 19","y":1840,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/N.H./11/19/","by":{"0":"30"}},{"t":"case","label":"Baker v. City of McKinney","cite":"601 F. Supp. 3d 124","y":2022,"court":"E.D. Tex.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.%20Supp.%203d/601/124/","by":{"0":"39","3":"6"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bell v. Hood","cite":"327 U.S. 678","y":1946,"id":"scdb/1945-042","sc":1,"ar":1,"sum":"1. Where the complaint seeks recovery squarely on the ground of violation of plaintiffs' rights under the Fourth and Fifth Amendments, a federal district court has jurisdiction of a suit against agents of the Federal Government to recover damages in excess of $3,000 alleged to have been suffered by the plaintiffs as a result of such violations-even though neither the Constitution nor the Congress has provided for …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"35"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bivens v. Six Unknown Named Agents","cite":"403 U.S. 388","y":1971,"id":"1970/301","sc":1,"ar":2,"sum":"The Supreme Court held that Bivens does have a cause of action for damages arising from the federal agents Fourth Amendment violations. Bivens must provide proof of his injuries in order to recover. The Court did not reach the privilege question because the Court of Appeals did not consider the question.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"6","1":"10","2":"8, 11, 27, 28, 30","3":"18, 19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Boise Cascade Corp. v. State ex rel. Or. State Bd. of Forestry","cite":"991 P.2d 563","y":1999,"court":"Or. Ct. App.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/P.2d/991/563/","by":{"0":"39","3":"6, 21–22"}},{"t":"case","label":"Borough of Duryea v. Guarnieri","cite":"564 U.S. 379","y":2011,"id":"2010/09-1476","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that state and local government employees may not sue their employers for retaliation under the First Amendment's Petition Clause when they petition the government on matters of private concern.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"36"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bowen v. Massachusetts","cite":"487 U.S. 879","y":1988,"id":"1987/87-712","sc":1,"sum":"The federal contribution (referred to as a \"reimbursement\") to a State's Medicaid program takes the form of advances based on the State's estimate of its future expenditures for covered services. Overpayments may be withheld from future advances, or, if a disallowance dispute develops, may be retained by the State at its option pending resolution of the dispute. After Massachusetts was reimbursed by the Department …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"16","2":"39"}},{"t":"case","label":"Cedar Point Nursery v. Hassid","cite":"141 S. Ct. 2063","y":2021,"id":"2020/20-107","sc":1,"ar":2,"sum":"A California regulation granting labor organizations a “right to take access” to an agricultural employer’s property to solicit support for unionization constitutes a per se physical taking.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"0":"14","2":"33"}},{"t":"case","label":"Chi., Burlington & Quincy R.R. Co. v. City of Chicago","cite":"166 U.S. 226","y":1897,"id":"1850-1900/166us226","sc":1,"ar":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Due Process clause required the states to award just compensation when taking private property for public use. While the Court warned of the danger of a government that did not have any restraints in exercising eminent domain, fair compensation could address that danger.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"29","1":"8","2":"20","3":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"City of Baytown v. Schrock","cite":"645 S.W.3d 174","y":2022,"court":"Tex.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.W.3d/645/174/","op":1,"ar":5,"sum":"Recognizing that it can be “proper” for Texas’s “common-law courts to create causes of action when federal tribunals should not”.","sumSrc":"brief:2","by":{"0":"39, 42, 43","2":"5","3":"12"}},{"t":"case","label":"City of Elgin v. Eaton","cite":"83 Ill. 535","y":1876,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Ill./83/535/","sum":"Stating that “the city became liable to an action” “after the adoption of our present constitution”.","sumSrc":"brief:1","by":{"0":"32","1":"31"}},{"t":"case","label":"City of Monterey v. Del Monte Dunes at Monterey, Ltd.","cite":"526 U.S. 687","y":1999,"id":"1998/97-1235","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held, in an opinion authored by Justice Anthony M. Kennedy, that property owners who file a Section 1983 civil rights suit seeking compensation for an alleged taking of their property can have a jury trial in some circumstances. \"[T]he disputed questions were whether the government had denied a constitutional right outside the bounds of its authority, and, if so, the extent of any resulting damages.\" Justice Kennedy then added: \"These were questions for the jury.\".","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"14","1":"27","2":"39, 40"}},{"t":"case","label":"Clayburg v. City of Chicago","cite":"25 Ill. 535","y":1861,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Ill./25/535/","by":{"0":"33","3":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Cuyahoga River Power Co. v. City of Akron","cite":"240 U.S. 462","y":1916,"id":"scdb/1915-120","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"As the bill in this case states that a municipality does not intend to institute proceedings to condemn, but does intend to take plaintiff’s property rights without compensation, and has taken steps that will destroy such rights, and that in so doing it purports to be acting under an ordinance which violates the contract clause of, and the Fourteenth Amendment to, the Federal Constitution, held that such municipal …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"29","1":"30","2":"40"}},{"t":"case","label":"Del., Lackawanna & W. R.R. Co. v. Town of Morristown","cite":"276 U.S. 182","y":1928,"id":"scdb/1927-130","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The railroad company constructed a driveway over its station grounds to connect with the streets of the town. The railroad and the town agreed that the driveway should be kept open and that the town should exercise upon the station grounds, etc., all necessary police powers for the regulation of traffic and for the enforcement of the railroad’s rules and regulations. The railroad granted a cabman exclusive right to …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"29","1":"30","2":"40"}},{"t":"case","label":"Dohany v. Rogers","cite":"281 U.S. 362","y":1930,"id":"scdb/1929-051","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"that 'the taking of private land to he so exchanged is a taking for a 'public purpose.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"29","1":"30","2":"40"}},{"t":"case","label":"Eaton v. Bos. Concord & Montreal R.R.","cite":"51 N.H. 504","y":1872,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/N.H./51/504/","by":{"0":"32","1":"31","3":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"First Union Nat’l Bank v. Hi Ho Mall Shopping Ventures, Inc.","cite":"869 A.2d 1193","y":2005,"court":"Conn.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/A.2d/869/1193/","by":{"0":"40"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ford Motor Credit Co. v. Milhollin","cite":"444 U.S. 555","y":1980,"id":"1979/78-1487","sc":1,"sum":"TILA does not mandate a general rule of disclosure for acceleration clauses.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"ii"}},{"t":"case","label":"Free Enterprise Fund v. Public Company Accounting Oversight Board","cite":"561 U.S. 477","y":2010,"id":"2009/08-861","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Sarbanes-Oxley Act violated the Appointment Clause of the Constitution.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"35","2":"41"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gardner v. Vill. of Newburgh","cite":"2 Johns. Ch. 162","y":1816,"court":"N.Y. Ch.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Johns.%20Ch./2/162/","by":{"0":"22","3":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gedney v. Inhabitants of Tewsbury","cite":"3 Mass. 307","y":1807,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Mass./3/307/","ar":1,"by":{"0":"31","2":"45"}},{"t":"case","label":"Greenway Dev. Co. v. Borough of Paramus","cite":"750 A.2d 764","y":2000,"court":"N.J.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/A.2d/750/764/","by":{"0":"39"}},{"t":"case","label":"Harman v. City of Omaha","cite":"23 N.W. 503","y":1885,"court":"Neb.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/N.W./23/503/","sum":"Stating that “the city became liable to an action” “after the adoption of our present constitution”.","sumSrc":"brief:1","by":{"0":"32","1":"31"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hernandez v. Mesa","cite":"140 S. Ct. 735","y":2020,"id":"2019/17-1678","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the family of a Mexican teenager who was killed by a U.S. Border Patrol agent in a cross-border shooting cannot sue the agent for damages under the U.S. Constitution.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"32","1":"32","2":"6, 28, 30"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hooe v. United States","cite":"218 U.S. 322","y":1910,"id":"scdb/1910-012","sc":1,"sum":"Congress, proceeding under the Constitution, declares what amount shall be drawn from the Treasury in pursuance of an appropriation.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"17","1":"19","2":"25-26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Horne v. Dep’t of Agric.","cite":"576 U.S. 350","y":2015,"url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/576/350/","sc":1,"by":{"0":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Jacobs v. United States","cite":"290 U.S. 13","y":1933,"id":"scdb/1933-035","sc":1,"sum":"1. The obligation of the United States to pay just compensation for private property taken under its power of eminent domain rests upon the Fifth Amendment, independent of statute or express promise.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"8, 13, 30, 39","1":"24","2":"38","3":"3, 13, 16, 17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Kelo v. City of New London","cite":"545 U.S. 469","y":2005,"id":"2004/04-108","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the city's taking of private property to sell for private development qualified as a \"public use\" under the takings clause of the Fifth Amendment.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"14, 15, 30, 31, 33, 38, 40, 44, 42"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lambert v. City & County of San Francisco","cite":"529 U.S. 1045","y":2000,"url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/529/1045/","sc":1,"by":{"0":"44"}},{"t":"case","label":"Langford v. United States","cite":"101 U.S. 341","y":1879,"id":"scdb/1879-177","sc":1,"by":{"0":"28","1":"18, 19","2":"19, 26","3":"12"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lapides v. Bd. of Regents of Univ. Sys. of Ga.","cite":"535 U.S. 613","y":2002,"id":"2001/01-298","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that a State waives its Eleventh Amendment immunity when it removes a case from state court to federal court.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lawyer v. Hilton Head Pub. Serv. Dist. No. 1","cite":"220 F.3d 298","y":2000,"court":"4th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/220/298/","sum":"Noting that Azul-Pacifico was “in apparent conflict with First English”.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"40"}},{"t":"case","label":"Little Rock Junction Ry. v. Woodruff","cite":"5 S.W. 792","y":1887,"court":"Ark.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.W./5/792/","by":{"0":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Me. Cmty. Health Options v. United States","cite":"140 S. Ct. 1308","y":2020,"id":"2019/18-1023","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the federal government has an obligation to pay the insurance carriers in this case for losses incurred under the \"Risk Corridors\" program of the Affordable Care Act as a result of their participation in the health care marketplace.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"15, 16, 17","1":"11, 20-22","2":"1, 7, 15, 38, 43-44","3":"7–9, 13–14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Mann v. Haigh","cite":"120 F.3d 34","y":1997,"court":"4th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/120/34/","by":{"0":"39","3":"6"}},{"t":"case","label":"Manning v. Mining & Mins. Div. of the Energy, Min. & Nat’l Res. Dep’t","cite":"144 P.3d 87","y":2006,"court":"N.M.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/P.3d/144/87/","by":{"0":"14, 39, 43","3":"6"}},{"t":"case","label":"McClenachan v. Curwen","cite":"3 Yeates 362","y":1802,"court":"Pa.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Yeates/3/362/","by":{"0":"31"}},{"t":"case","label":"Miss. & Rum River Boom Co. v. Patterson","cite":"98 U.S. 403","y":1878,"id":"scdb/1878-111","sc":1,"sum":"The plaintiff is a corporation created by the laws of Minnesota, known as the Mississippi and Rum River Boom Company, and the defendant is a citizen of the State of Illinois.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"32","1":"31"}},{"t":"case","label":"N. Transp. Co. v. City of Chicago","cite":"99 U.S. 635","y":1878,"id":"scdb/1878-127","sc":1,"sum":"This is an action of trespass on the case by the Northern Transportation Company of Ohio against Chicago, 111., to recover damages sustained by reason of the construction by that city of a tunnel under the Chicago River along the line of La Salle Street. The company offered evidence tending to prove that it possessed a certain lot in Chicago, with dock and wharfing rights and privileges; that it owned a line of …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"32","1":"31"}},{"t":"case","label":"N.Y. State Rifle & Pistol Ass’n v. Bruen","cite":"142 S. Ct. 2111","y":2022,"id":"2021/20-843","sc":1,"sum":"New York’s proper-cause requirement for obtaining an unrestricted license to carry a concealed firearm violates the Fourteenth Amendment in that it prevents law-abiding citizens with ordinary self-defense needs from exercising their Second Amendment right to keep and bear arms.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"0":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"Nw. Austin Mun. Util. Dist. No. 1 v. Holder","cite":"557 U.S. 193","y":2009,"id":"2008/08-322","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Voting Rights Act (VRA) permits all political subdivisions to seek bailout from the preclearance requirements of the VRA.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"36"}},{"t":"case","label":"O’Connor v. Eubanks","cite":"83 F.4th 1018","y":2023,"court":"6th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.4th/83/1018/","sum":"Noting Judge Oldham’s “thoughtful opinion . . . collecting over a century of Supreme Court cases suggesting plaintiffs have a cause of action directly under the Takings Clause[.]”.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"19","3":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Payne v. Tennessee","cite":"501 U.S. 808","y":1991,"id":"1990/90-5721","sc":1,"sum":"The Court reasoned that since \"[v]irtually no limits are placed on the relevant mitigating evidence a capital defendant may introduce concerning his own circumstances,\" the prosecution must be allowed to submit similar counter evidence.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"41"}},{"t":"case","label":"People ex rel. Utley v. Hayden","cite":"6 Hill 359","y":1844,"court":"N.Y. Sup. Ct.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Hill/6/359/","sum":"Holding that mandamus is the appropriate remedy when appraisers refuse to undertake duty to assess damages for taking.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"30","2":"44"}},{"t":"case","label":"Pumpelly v. Green Bay Co.","cite":"80 U.S. 166","y":1871,"id":"scdb/1871-095","sc":1,"sum":"1. Where a plea relies on a statute authority as a defence, it must allege the facts which it asserts to be so authorized, and cannot pleud generally that it complied with the statute. Hence a plea is bad which states that defendant raised the water in a luke no higher than the statute authorized, when the State forbid the water being raised above its ordinary level.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"32"}},{"t":"case","label":"Reardon v. City & County of San Francisco","cite":"6 P. 317","y":1885,"court":"Cal.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/P./6/317/","by":{"0":"32","1":"31"}},{"t":"case","label":"San Diego Gas & Electric Co. v. City of San Diego","cite":"450 U.S. 621","y":1981,"id":"1980/79-678","sc":1,"ar":1,"sum":"Since 28 U. S. C. § 1257 permits this Court to review only \"[f]inal judgments or decrees\" of a state court, the appeal must be dismissed because of the absence of a final judgment. While the Court of Appeal decided that monetary compensation is not an appropriate remedy, it did not decide whether any other remedy is available because it has not decided whether any taking, in fact, occurred but appeared to have …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"43"}},{"t":"case","label":"SDDS, Inc. v. State","cite":"650 N.W.2d 1","y":2002,"court":"S.D.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/N.W.2d/650/1/","sum":"Recognizing that First English means “the [compensation] remedy does not depend on statutory facilitation”.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"14, 39","3":"6"}},{"t":"case","label":"Seaboard Air Line Ry. Co. v. United States","cite":"261 U.S. 299","y":1923,"id":"scdb/1922-119","sc":1,"sum":"1. In an action against the United States under § 10 of the Lever Act, to recover just compensation for property requisitioned for public use, the owner is entitled to judgment for the value of the property as of the time ofthe taking and for so much in addition as will produce a full equivalent of that value, paid contemporaneously with the taking.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"30, 33","1":"27","2":"40"}},{"t":"case","label":"Speed v. Mills","cite":"919 F. Supp. 2d 122","y":2013,"court":"D.D.C.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.%20Supp.%202d/919/122/","by":{"0":"39","3":"6"}},{"t":"case","label":"Stop the Beach Renourishment, Inc. v. Fla. Dep’t of Env’t Prot.","cite":"560 U.S. 702","y":2010,"id":"2009/08-1151","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that a ruling on littoral (\"on or near the shore\") takings by the Florida Supreme Court did not cause a judicial takings violation of the Fifth or Fourteenth Amendments.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Thayer v. Boston, 36 Mass. (19 Pick.) 511 (1837). 30 Timbs v. Indiana","cite":"139 S. Ct. 682","y":2019,"id":"2018/17-1091","sc":1,"sum":"The Eighth Amendment’s Excessive Fines Clause is an incorporated protection applicable to the States under the Fourteenth Amendment’s Due Process Clause.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"0":"18, 26","3":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Causby","cite":"328 U.S. 256","y":1946,"id":"1940-1955/328us256","sc":1,"sum":"The Court concluded that the ancient common law doctrine \"has no place in the modern world.\" Justice Douglas noted that, were the Court to accept the doctrine as valid, \"every transcontinental flight would subject the operator to countless trespass suits.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"15","1":"19, 20","3":"7, 17"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Clarke","cite":"445 U.S. 253","y":1980,"id":"1979/78-1693","sc":1,"sum":"Title 25 U. S. C. § 357, which provides that lands allotted in severalty to Indians may be \"condemned\" for any public purpose under the laws of the State or Territory where located, does not authorize a state or local government to \"condemn\" allotted Indian trust lands by physical occupation. Under the \"plain meaning\" canon of statutory construction, the term \"condemned\" in § 357 refers to a formal condemnation …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"13","1":"24, 27"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Russell","cite":"80 U.S. 623","y":1871,"id":"scdb/1871-042","sc":1,"sum":"By the act of Congress of 1855, constituting the said court, jurisdiction is given to it to hear and determine all claims against the United States, “founded on any law of Congress, or upon any regulation of an executive department, or upon any contract., expi’ess or implied, with the government of the United States.”","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"28","3":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Testan","cite":"424 U.S. 392","y":1976,"id":"1975/74-753","sc":1,"sum":"Respondent Government trial attorneys with civil service grade GS-13 classifications requested their employing agency to reclassify their positions to grade GS-14, contending that their duties and responsibilities met the requirements for the higher grade and were identical to those of other trial attorneys classified as GS-14 in another agency, and that under the principle of \"equal pay for substantially equal …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"15","1":"20, 22","2":"43"}},{"t":"case","label":"Vill. of Euclid v. Ambler Realty Co.","cite":"272 U.S. 365","y":1926,"id":"1900-1940/272us365","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court held that a Euclid, Ohio, zoning ordinance was not an unreasonable extension of the village's police power and was not arbitrary, and thus it did not violate the Due Process and Equal Protection Clauses of the Fourteenth Amendment.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"29","1":"30","2":"40"}},{"t":"case","label":"Vill. of Norwood v. Baker","cite":"172 U.S. 269","y":1898,"id":"scdb/1898-031","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The principle underlying special assessments upon private property to meet the cost of public improvements is that the property upon which they are imposed is peculiarly benefited, and therefore that the owners do not in fact pay anything in excess of what they receive by reason of such improvement.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"29","1":"30","2":"40-41"}},{"t":"case","label":"Whitcomb v. Chavis","cite":"403 U.S. 124","y":1971,"id":"1970/92","sc":1,"sum":"The judgment is reversed and the case remanded. Pp. 140-170; 179-180. 305 F. Supp. 1364, reversed and remanded. WHITCOMB v. CHAVIS","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"0":"ii"}},{"t":"case","label":"Williamson County Regional Planning Commission v. Hamilton Bank of Johnson City","cite":"473 U.S. 172","y":1985,"id":"1984/84-4","sc":1,"sum":"The Supreme Court held that even if there was a temporary taking, a decision on money damages was premature because Hamilton Bank had not obtained a final administrative decision on the subdivision layout. The claim was premature under the Due Process Clause of the Fourteenth Amendment for the same reason. Justice Byron R. White dissented from the holding that the issues in the case were not ripe for decision.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"0":"40","2":"39"}},{"t":"case","label":"Young v. McKenzie","cite":"3 Ga. 31","y":1847,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Ga./3/31/","sum":"Holding that the just-compensation principle dates to Magna Carta and is an inherent limit on the power of all governments, whether or not their constitutions contain a just-compensation clause.","sumSrc":"brief:0","by":{"0":"21"}},{"t":"const","label":"Amendment V","clauses":[["grand-jury","Grand Jury Clause"],["double-jeopardy","Double Jeopardy Clause"],["self-incrimination","Self-Incrimination Clause"],["due-process-5","Due Process Clause (Fifth Amendment)"],["takings","Takings Clause"]],"text":"No person shall be held to answer for a capital, or otherwise infamous crime, unless on a presentment or indictment of a Grand Jury, except in cases arising in the land or naval forces, or in the Militia, when in actual service in time of War or public danger; nor shall any person be subject for the same offence to be twice put in jeopardy of life or limb; nor shall be compelled in any criminal case to be a witness against himself, nor be deprived of life, liberty, or property, without due process of law; nor shall private property be taken for public use, without just compensation.","by":{"0":"1, 10, 19, 35","1":"3-10, 13-17, 19, 21, 23-30, 32","3":"1–3, 6–20, 22, 23"}},{"t":"const","label":"Amendment XIV","clauses":[["citizenship-clause","Citizenship Clause"],["privileges-or-immunities","Privileges or Immunities Clause"],["due-process-14","Due Process Clause (Fourteenth Amendment)"],["equal-protection","Equal Protection Clause"],["disqualification","Disqualification Clause (Section 3)"],["enforcement-14","Enforcement Clause (Section 5)"]],"by":{"0":"1, 9, 10, 18","1":"4, 7-10","3":"8, 14"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 1254","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1254","subs":["(1)"],"by":{"0":"1"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 1292","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1292","subs":["(b)"],"stext":{"head":"§1292. Interlocutory decisions","paras":[[0,"(a) Except as provided in subsections (c) and (d) of this section, the courts of appeals shall have jurisdiction of appeals from:"],[1,"(1) Interlocutory orders of the district courts of the United States, the United States District Court for the District of the Canal Zone, the District Court of Guam, and the District Court of the Virgin Islands, or of the judges thereof, granting, continuing, modifying, refusing or dissolving injunctions, or refusing to dissolve or modify injunctions, except where a direct review may be had in the Supreme Court;"],[1,"(2) Interlocutory orders appointing receivers, or refusing orders to wind up receiverships or to take steps to accomplish the purposes thereof, such as directing sales or other disposals of property;"],[1,"(3) Interlocutory decrees of such district courts or the judges thereof determining the rights and liabilities of the parties to admiralty cases in which appeals from final decrees are allowed."],[0,"(b) When a district judge, in making in a civil action an order not otherwise appealable under this section, shall be of the opinion that such order involves a controlling question of law as to which there is substantial ground for difference of opinion and that an immediate appeal from the order may materially advance the ultimate termination of the litigation, he shall so state in writing in such order. The Court of Appeals which would have jurisdiction of an appeal of such action may thereupon, in its discretion, permit an appeal to be taken from such order, if application is made to it within ten days after the entry of the order: Provided, however, That application for an appeal hereunder shall not stay proceedings in the district court unless the district judge or the Court of Appeals or a judge thereof shall so order."],[0,"(c) The United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit shall have exclusive jurisdiction—"],[1,"(1) of an appeal from an interlocutory order or decree described in subsection (a) or (b) of this section in any case over which the court would have jurisdiction of an appeal under section 1295 of this title; and"],[1,"(2) of an appeal from a judgment in a civil action for patent infringement which would otherwise be appealable to the United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit and is final except for an accounting."],[0,"(d)(1) When the chief judge of the Court of International Trade issues an order under the provisions of section 256(b) of this title, or when any judge of the Court of International Trade, in issuing any other interlocutory order, includes in the order a statement that a controlling question of law is involved with respect to which there is a substantial ground for difference of opinion and that an immediate appeal from that order may materially advance the ultimate termination of the litigation, the United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit may, in its discretion, permit an appeal to be taken from such order, if application is made to that Court within ten days after the entry of such order."],[0,"(2) When the chief judge of the United States Court of Federal Claims issues an order under section 798(b) of this title, or when any judge of the United States Court of Federal Claims, in issuing an interlocutory order, includes in the order a statement that a controlling question of law is involved with respect to which there is a substantial ground for difference of opinion and that an immediate appeal from that order may materially advance the ultimate termination of the litigation, the United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit may, in its discretion, permit an appeal to be taken from such order, if application is made to that Court within ten days after the entry of such order."],[0,"(3) Neither the application for nor the granting of an appeal under this subsection shall stay proceedings in the Court of International Trade or in the Court of Federal Claims, as the case may be, unless a stay is ordered by a judge of the Court of International Trade or of the Court of Federal Claims or by the United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit or a judge of that court."],[0,"(4)(A) The United States Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit shall have exclusive jurisdiction of an appeal from an interlocutory order of a district court of the United States, the District Court of Guam, the District Court of the Virgin Islands, or the District Court for the Northern Mariana Islands, granting or denying, in whole or in part, a motion to transfer an action to the United States Court of Federal Claims under section 1631 of this title."],[0,"(B) When a motion to transfer an action to the Court of Federal Claims is filed in a district court, no further proceedings shall be taken in the district court until 60 days after the court has ruled upon the motion. If an appeal is taken from the district court's grant or denial of the motion, proceedings shall be further stayed until the appeal has been decided by the Court of Appeals for the Federal Circuit. The stay of proceedings in the district court shall not bar the granting of preliminary or injunctive relief, where appropriate and where expedition is reasonably necessary. However, during the period in which proceedings are stayed as provided in this subparagraph, no transfer to the Court of Federal Claims pursuant to the motion shall be carried out."],[0,"(e) The Supreme Court may prescribe rules, in accordance with section 2072 of this title, to provide for an appeal of an interlocutory decision to the courts of appeals that is not otherwise provided for under subsection (a), (b), (c), or (d)."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/28/1292?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"head":"§1292. Interlocutory decisions","tname":"Judiciary and Judicial Procedure","by":{"0":"5","1":"3"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 1331","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1331","stext":{"head":"§1331. Federal question","paras":[[0,"The district courts shall have original jurisdiction of all civil actions arising under the Constitution, laws, or treaties of the United States."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/28/1331?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"ar":1,"head":"§1331. Federal question","tname":"Judiciary and Judicial Procedure","by":{"0":"16, 34","1":"9, 32","3":"21"}},{"t":"usc","label":"42 U.S.C. § 18062","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/18062","by":{"0":"16, 17"}},{"t":"usc","label":"42 U.S.C. § 1983","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/42/1983","stext":{"head":"§1983. Civil action for deprivation of rights","paras":[[0,"Every person who, under color of any statute, ordinance, regulation, custom, or usage, of any State or Territory or the District of Columbia, subjects, or causes to be subjected, any citizen of the United States or other person within the jurisdiction thereof to the deprivation of any rights, privileges, or immunities secured by the Constitution and laws, shall be liable to the party injured in an action at law, suit in equity, or other proper proceeding for redress, except that in any action brought against a judicial officer for an act or omission taken in such officer's judicial capacity, injunctive relief shall not be granted unless a declaratory decree was violated or declaratory relief was unavailable. For the purposes of this section, any Act of Congress applicable exclusively to the District of Columbia shall be considered to be a statute of the District of Columbia."]],"more":false,"ed":"2024","src":"https://www.govinfo.gov/link/uscode/42/1983?type=usc&year=mostrecent&link-type=html"},"op":4,"ar":3,"head":"§1983. Civil action for deprivation of rights","tname":"The Public Health and Welfare","by":{"0":"4","1":"3, 4, 9, 25, 28, 32","3":"4, 5, 20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Alden v. Maine","cite":"527 U.S. 706","y":1999,"id":"1998/98-436","sc":1,"op":2,"ar":1,"sum":"A sharply divided court held in a 5-4 decision that Congress may not use its Article I powers to abrogate the states' sovereign immunity. Both the terms and history of the eleventh amendment suggest that States are immune from suits in their own courts.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"9"}},{"t":"case","label":"(III) Cases—Continued: Alexander v. Sandoval","cite":"532 U.S. 275","y":2001,"id":"2000/99-1908","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that there is no private right of action to enforce disparate-impact regulations promulgated under Title VI. \"Title VI itself directly reaches only instances of intentional discrimination,\" wrote Justice Scalia, \"[n]either as originally enacted nor as later amended does Title VI display an intent to create a freestanding private right of action to enforce regulations promulgated under [section 602].\" Justice John Paul Stevens filed a dissenting opinion, which was join by Justices David H. Souter, Ruth Bader Ginsburg, and Stephen G. Breyer.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"21","2":"2, 28, 30"}},{"t":"case","label":"Arizona v. Navajo Nation","cite":"599 U.S. 555","y":2023,"id":"2022/21-1484","sc":1,"sum":"The 1868 treaty establishing the Navajo Reservation reserved necessary water to accomplish the purpose of the Navajo Reservation but did not require the United States to take affirmative steps to secure water for the Tribe.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"1":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"Armstrong v. Exceptional Child Ctr., Inc.","cite":"575 U.S. 320","y":2015,"id":"2014/14-15","sc":1,"sum":"A case that determines whether or not a private right of action exists under the Medicaid Act.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"8","2":"21, 22, 34","3":"13, 14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Babbitt v. Youpee","cite":"519 U.S. 234","y":1997,"id":"1996/95-1595","sc":1,"sum":"Authored by Justice Ruth Bader Ginsburg, The Court ruled that the amended Section 207 of the Indian Land Consolidation Act does not cure the constitutional deficiency The Court identified in the original version of Section 207, which amounted to a taking of private property without just compensation, in violation of the Fifth Amendment.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Block v. North Dakota","cite":"461 U.S. 273","y":1983,"id":"1982/81-2337","sc":1,"sum":"North Dakota filed suit in Federal District Court against several federal officials to resolve a dispute as to ownership of certain portions of a riverbed within the State. The United States claims title to most of the disputed area on the basis of its status as a riparian landowner on a nonnavigable river, while the State asserts that the river was navigable when North Dakota was admitted to the Union in 1889 and …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"California v. Arizona","cite":"440 U.S. 59","y":1979,"id":"1978/78_orig","sc":1,"ar":2,"sum":"Under § 2409a (a), the United States has waived its sovereign immunity to suit in this case, and hence there is no bar to the suit. The legislative history of § 1346 (f) shows no intent by Congress to divest this Court of jurisdiction over such actions in cases otherwise within its original jurisdiction, an attempt that would raise grave constitutional questions. The section did no more than assure that such …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"City of Boerne v. Flores","cite":"521 U.S. 507","y":1997,"id":"1996/95-2074","sc":1,"sum":"The Court ruled that Congress exceeded its Fourteenth Amendment powers when it enacted the Religious Freedom Restoration Act, subjecting local ordinance to federal regulation.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"26","3":"14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Connolly v. Pension Benefit Guar. Corp.","cite":"475 U.S. 211","y":1986,"id":"1985/84-1555","sc":1,"sum":"The withdrawal liability provisions of the MPPAA do not violate the Taking Clause.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"DHS v. Thuraissigiam","cite":"140 S. Ct. 1959","y":2020,"id":"2019/19-161","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that 8 U.S.C. § 1252(e)(2) as applied to the respondent Thuraissigiam does not violate the Suspension Clause of the Constitution.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"30"}},{"t":"case","label":"Davis v. Passman","cite":"442 U.S. 228","y":1979,"id":"1978/78-5072","sc":1,"ar":1,"sum":"Relying on Bivens v. Six Unknown Federal Narcotics Agents (1971) and Butz v. Economou (1978), the Court reversed the lower court's conclusions. Both cases affirmed a citizen's right to bring suit against federal officers for constitutional violations. In this case, Passman violated Davis's rights through sexual discrimination.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"9, 14","3":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Eastern Enters. v. Apfel","cite":"524 U.S. 498","y":1998,"id":"1997/97-42","sc":1,"ar":1,"sum":"Currently unknown.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Eastport Steamship Corp. v. United States","cite":"372 F.2d 1002","y":1967,"court":"Ct. Cl.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.2d/372/1002/","by":{"1":"20","2":"43"}},{"t":"case","label":"Egbert v. Boule","cite":"142 S. Ct. 1793","y":2022,"id":"2021/21-147","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The authority of a court to imply a cause of action under Bivens v. Six Unknown Fed. Narcotics Agents, 403 U. S. 388, does not extend to either Boule’s Fourth Amendment excessive-force claim or his First Amendment retaliation claim.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"1":"10, 32","2":"2, 7, 8, 9, 28","3":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"FDIC v. Meyer","cite":"510 U.S. 471","y":1994,"id":"1993/92-741","sc":1,"sum":"After the Federal Savings and Loan Insurance Corporation (FSLIC), as receiver for a failing thrift institution, terminated respondent Meyer from his job as a senior officer of that institution, he filed this suit in the District Court, claiming that his summary discharge deprived him of a property right without due process of law in violation of the Fifth Amendment. In making this claim, he relied on Bivens v. Six …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"9, 29"}},{"t":"case","label":"Felker v. Turpin","cite":"518 U.S. 651","y":1996,"id":"1995/95-8836","sc":1,"sum":"The unanimous Court held that the Act's creation of an appellate panel, charged with reviewing all second or successive habeas applications, is not unconstitutional. The Act simply transfers the duty of habeas review from the district courts to an appellate panel.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"30"}},{"t":"case","label":"First English Evangelical Lutheran Church v. County of Los Angeles","cite":"482 U.S. 304","y":1987,"id":"1986/85-1199","sc":1,"op":2,"sum":"The Court held that the ordinance violated the Constitution. Noting that the fate of the Church's property had been in limbo for over six years (the suit which it had filed in 1979 had been denied a hearing as late as October of 1985), Chief Justice Rehnquist argued that, because the church was unable to use its property during this time, a \"taking\" of the property had occurred.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"6, 23-26","2":"9, 12, 34, 35, 36, 37, 38, 39, 45, 37, 36","3":"1–3, 5, 9, 12–14, 16, 17, 19, 21, 1–2, 4–5, 9–10, 15, 20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Glidden Co. v. Zdanok","cite":"370 U.S. 530","y":1962,"id":"1961/242","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that judges in the United States Court of Claims and the United States Court of Customs and Patent Appeals constitutionally protected in tenure and compensation under Article III of the Constitution.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"11"}},{"t":"case","label":"Goodyear Tire & Rubber Co. v. United States","cite":"276 U.S. 287","y":1928,"id":"scdb/1927-051","sc":1,"sum":"1. A lease to the United States for a term of years, made without any specific authority of law, and entered into when there was no appropriation available for the payment of rent after the first fiscal year, does not bind the Government after that year. Rev. Stats. §§ 3732, 3679. Letter v. United States, 271 U. S. 204.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"Horne v. Department of Agric.","cite":"569 U.S. 513","y":2013,"id":"2012/12-123","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held the Takings Clause must be evaluated under the capacity in which the Government fines, and the citizens do not need to file a Takings Clause challenge in the Court of Federal Claims.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Knick v. Township of Scott","cite":"139 S. Ct. 2162","y":2019,"id":"2018/17-647","sc":1,"op":2,"ar":15,"sum":"A government violates the Takings Clause when it takes property without compensation, and a property owner may bring a Fifth Amendment claim under 42 U. S. C. §1983 at that time; the state-litigation requirement of Williamson County Regional Planning Comm’n v. Hamilton Bank of Johnson City, 473 U. S. 172, is overruled.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"1":"4, 7, 8, 15-18, 28, 31, 32","2":"7, 9, 30, 33, 39, 45","3":"7, 8, 13, 15, 16, 18–19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lane v. Pena","cite":"518 U.S. 187","y":1996,"id":"1995/95-365","sc":1,"sum":"Although the related provisions of the 1973 Rehabilitation Act and the 1991 Civil Rights Act might in combination be read as waiving the federal government's sovereign immunity, absent an unambiguous waiver of sovereign immunity, the court cannot permit damages to be awarded.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"12, 14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Library of Congress v. Shaw","cite":"478 U.S. 310","y":1986,"id":"1985/85-54","sc":1,"sum":"The no-interest rule applies here so as to preclude the award of increased compensation to respondent's counsel for the delay in receiving payment for his services.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"15","2":"23"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lynch v. United States","cite":"292 U.S. 571","y":1934,"id":"scdb/1933-150","sc":1,"sum":"1. Policies of yearly renewable term insurance issued under the War Risk Insurance Act, are not gratuities but are contracts of the United States.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"11","2":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"Malone v. Bowdoin","cite":"369 U.S. 643","y":1962,"id":"1961/113","sc":1,"sum":"The action was one against the United States and, in the absence of consent by the United States, the District Court was without jurisdiction. Larson v. Domestic & Foreign Commerce Corp., 337 U. S. 682. Pp. 643-648. 284 F. 2d 95, reversed.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Medellin v. Texas","cite":"552 U.S. 491","y":2008,"id":"2007/06-984","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the signed Protocol of the Vienna Convention did not make the treaty self-executing and, therefore, the treaty is not binding upon state courts until it is enacted into law by Congress.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"26"}},{"t":"case","label":"OPM v. Richmond","cite":"496 U.S. 414","y":1990,"id":"1989/88-1943","sc":1,"ar":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Appropriations Clause of the Constitution explicitly states that no money can be paid from the Treasury unless specifically authorized by a statute. Since there is a statute that denies Richmond his benefits, and there is not one that provides an exception, the Court held that Richmond’s receipt of incorrect information cannot affect the non-payment of his benefits.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"12, 14","2":"18, 19, 22, 25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ruckelshaus v. Monsanto Co.","cite":"467 U.S. 986","y":1984,"id":"1983/83-196","sc":1,"ar":1,"sum":"The Federal Insecticide, Fungicide, and Rodenticide Act (FIFRA) authorizes the Environmental Protection Agency (EPA) to use data submitted by an applicant for registration of a covered product (hereinafter pesticide) in evaluating the application of a subsequent applicant, and to disclose publicly some of the submitted data. Under the dataconsideration provisions of § 3, as amended in 1978, applicants now are …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"8"}},{"t":"case","label":"Schillinger v. United States","cite":"155 U.S. 163","y":1894,"id":"scdb/1894-019","sc":1,"sum":"The United States cannot be sued in their courts without their consent.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"18, 19","2":"19, 25, 26"}},{"t":"case","label":"Stoneridge Inv. Partners, LLC v. Scientific-Atlanta, Inc.","cite":"552 U.S. 148","y":2008,"id":"2007/06-43","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that, under Central Bank and Section 10(b), securities fraud plaintiffs cannot sue \"aiders and abettors\" like Scientific-Atlanta. The Court noted Congress' subsequent failure to create a right of private action against aiders and abettors in the Private Securities Litigation Reform Act for support of its position that no such right should be allowed in the present case.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"21"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Bormes","cite":"568 U.S. 6","y":2012,"id":"2012/11-192","sc":1,"ar":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Little Tucker Act does not apply to lawsuits brought under the Federal Credit Reporting Act.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"18, 20, 21"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Great Falls Mfg. Co.","cite":"112 U.S. 645","y":1884,"id":"scdb/1884-073","sc":1,"sum":"Where property to which the United States asserts no title, is taken by their officers or agents, pursuant to an act of Congress, as private property, for the public use, the government is under an implied obligation to-make just compensation to the owner.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"19","2":"25","3":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Idaho","cite":"508 U.S. 1","y":1993,"id":"1992/92-190","sc":1,"sum":"The McCarran Amendment does not waive the United States' sovereign immunity from fees of the kind sought by Idaho. While \"fees\" and \"costs\" generally mean two different things in the context of lawsuits, the line is blurred, indeed, in the context of this proceeding. UNITED STATES v. IDAHO EX REL. DIRECTOR, IDAHO DEPT. OF WATER RESOURCES Whereas Idaho courts used to proportionately tax the \"costs\" against all …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"14"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Lee","cite":"106 U.S. 196","y":1882,"id":"scdb/1882-052","sc":1,"by":{"1":"16","3":"15"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Mitchell","cite":"463 U.S. 206","y":1983,"id":"1982/81-1748","sc":1,"sum":"The United States is accountable in money damages for alleged breaches of trust in connection with its management of forest resources on allotted lands of the Quinault Reservation.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"15, 20","2":"23, 25, 26"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Navajo Nation","cite":"556 U.S. 287","y":2009,"id":"2008/07-1410","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that its decision in Navajo I did not terminate the defendant's claim, however, the defendant did not provide sufficient sources of law and therefore the suit should be dismissed.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"20, 22","3":"9"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. Sherwood","cite":"312 U.S. 584","y":1941,"id":"scdb/1940-058","sc":1,"sum":"A New York court, acting under authority of § 795 of the New York Civil Practice Act, made an order authorizing a judgment creditor to sue under the Tucker Act, to recover damages from the United States for breach of its contract with the. judgment debtor, the order directing that out of the recovery the judgment creditor should be entitled to a sum sufficient to satisfy his judgment with interest, costs, etc. The …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"12","2":"19, 25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Virginia Office for Prot. & Advocacy v. Stewart","cite":"563 U.S. 247","y":2011,"id":"2010/09-529","sc":1,"op":1,"sum":"The Court held that a federal court may hear a lawsuit for prospective injunctive relief against state officials brought by another agency of the same state.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"1":"9"}},{"t":"case","label":"Will v. Michigan Dep’t of State Police","cite":"491 U.S. 58","y":1989,"id":"1988/87-1207","sc":1,"sum":"Neither States nor state officials acting in their official capacities are \"persons\" within meaning of § 1983.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"9","2":"10, 18"}},{"t":"case","label":"Williams v. United States","cite":"289 U.S. 553","y":1933,"id":"scdb/1932-134","sc":1,"ar":1,"sum":"1. The judicial power of the Court of Claims is not vested in virtue of Art. III of the Constitution, so as to bring its judges within the protection of that Article as to tenure of office and compensation. Ex parte Bakelite Corp., 279 U.S. 438. Expressions in United States v. Klein, 13 Wall. 128, and other cases criticized. Pp. 567, 568, 581.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"1":"11-13, 20"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 1491","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1491","subs":["(a)(1)"],"by":{"1":"18, 19"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 1441","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1441","subs":["(a)"],"by":{"1":"2"}},{"t":"case","label":"Allen v. Cooper","cite":"140 S. Ct. 994","y":2020,"id":"2019/18-877","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Copyright Remedy Clarification Act was not a valid abrogation of state sovereign immunity to allow authors of original expression to sue states who infringe their federal copyrights.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"46"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ariyan, Inc. v. Sewerage & Water Bd. of New Orleans","cite":"29 F.4th 226","y":2022,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.4th/29/226/","by":{"2":"29"}},{"t":"case","label":"Barr v. Am. Ass’n of Pol. Consultants, Inc.","cite":"140 S. Ct. 2335","y":2020,"id":"2019/19-631","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that a provision of the Telephone Consumer Protection Act of 1991 creating an exception to the prohibition on automated calls for government debt collection calls violates the First Amendment but is severable from the remainder of the statute.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"21"}},{"t":"case","label":"Bd. of Tr. of Univ. of Ala. v. Garrett","cite":"531 U.S. 356","y":2001,"id":"2000/99-1240","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that suits in federal court by state employees to recover money damages by reason of the state's failure to comply with Title I of the ADA are barred by the Eleventh Amendment.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"27"}},{"t":"case","label":"Beekman v. Saratoga & Schenectady R.R.","cite":"3 Paige Ch. 45","y":1831,"court":"N.Y. Ch.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Paige%20Ch./3/45/","by":{"2":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Meyers ex rel. Benzing v. Texas","cite":"410 F.3d 236","y":2005,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/410/236/","by":{"2":"32"}},{"t":"case","label":"Brown v. De La Cruz","cite":"156 S.W.3d 560","y":2004,"court":"Tex.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.W.3d/156/560/","sum":"Recognizing that it can be “proper” for Texas’s “common-law courts to create causes of action when federal tribunals should not”.","sumSrc":"brief:2","by":{"2":"5"}},{"t":"case","label":"Church of Scientology of Cal. v. IRS","cite":"484 U.S. 9","y":1987,"id":"1987/86-472","sc":1,"sum":"The Haskell Amendment does not exempt from § 6103(b)(2)'s definition of confidential \"return information\" material in IRS files which can be redacted to delete those parts which would identify a particular taxpayer. If the mere removal of identifying details sufficed to put the information \"in a form\" envisioned by the Amendment, the remainder of the protected categories of information included in §6103(b)(2) would …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"25"}},{"t":"case","label":"Cincinnati Soap Co. v. United States","cite":"301 U.S. 308","y":1937,"id":"scdb/1936-117","sc":1,"sum":"Section 602 of the Revenue Act of 1934 imposes a tax of 3 cents per pound upon the first domestic processing of coconut oil, and provides that all such taxes collected with respect to coconut oil wholly of Philippine production, etc., \"shall be held as a separate fund and paid to the Treasury of the Philippine Islands, but if at any time the Philippine Government provides by any law for any subsidy to be paid to the …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"City of Milwaukee v. Illinois","cite":"451 U.S. 304","y":1981,"id":"1980/79-408","sc":1,"sum":"In original proceedings brought by respondent State of Illinois, alleging that petitioners-the city of Milwaukee, its Sewerage Commission, and Milwaukee County's Metropolitan Sewerage Commission-and other Wisconsin cities were polluting Lake Michigan because of overflows of untreated sewage from their sewer systems and discharges of inadequately treated sewage from their treatment plants, this Court recognized the …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"5"}},{"t":"case","label":"Collins v. Yellen","cite":"141 S. Ct. 1761","y":2021,"id":"2020/19-422","sc":1,"sum":"Because the Federal Housing Finance Agency (FHFA) did not exceed its authority under the Housing and Economic Recovery Act of 2008 as a conservator of Fannie Mae and Freddie Mac, the anti-injunction provisions of the Recovery Act bar the statutory claim brought by shareholders of those entities; the Recovery Act’s structure, which restricts the President’s power to remove the FHFA Director, violates the separation of powers.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"2":"21"}},{"t":"case","label":"Cooper Indus., Inc. v. Aviall Servs., Inc.","cite":"543 U.S. 157","y":2004,"id":"2004/02-1192","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that a private party, who, like Aviall, had not been sued under CERCLA could not obtain cleanup contributions from other liable parties. The Court reasoned that CERCLA's language made clear that parties could seek cleanup contribution only during or following a civil action.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"36, 37"}},{"t":"case","label":"Corr. Servs. Corp. v. Malesko","cite":"534 U.S. 61","y":2001,"id":"2001/00-860","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that Bivens' limited holding may not be extended to confer a right of action for damages against private entities acting under color of federal law. The Court reasoned that the threat of suit against an individual's employer was not the kind of deterrence contemplated by the Bivens decision.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"2"}},{"t":"case","label":"Erie R,R. Co. v. Tompkins","cite":"304 U.S. 64","y":1938,"id":"1900-1940/304us64","sc":1,"sum":"A case in which the Court overturned its prior decision in Swift v. Tyson and held that federal courts exercising diversity jurisdiction cannot create their own common law for issues that properly fall within state law.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"43, 44"}},{"t":"case","label":"Fitzpatrick v. Bitzer","cite":"427 U.S. 445","y":1976,"id":"1975/75-251","sc":1,"sum":"Present and retired male employees of the State of Connecticut (petitioners in No. 75-251) brought this class action alleging, inter alia, that certain provisions of the State's statutory retirement benefit plan discriminated against them because of their sex, in violation of Title VII of the Civil Rights Act of 1964, which, as amended, extends coverage to the States as employers. The District Court ruled in their …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"27"}},{"t":"case","label":"Franchise Tax Bd. of Cal. v. Hyatt","cite":"139 S. Ct. 1485","y":2019,"id":"2018/17-1299","sc":1,"sum":"Nevada v. Hall, 440 U. S. 410, is overruled; States retain their sovereign immunity from private suits brought in courts of other States.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"2":"20"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gomez-Perez v. Potter","cite":"553 U.S. 474","y":2008,"id":"2007/06-1321","sc":1,"sum":"Noting the similarity between these statutes and the ADEA, Alito concluded that Congress had intended to include a retaliation prohibition in the ADEA. Chief Justice John G. Roberts and Justice Clarence Thomas filed dissenting opinions each joined in part by Justice Antonin Scalia.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"33"}},{"t":"case","label":"Gutersloh v. Texas","cite":"25 F.3d 1044","y":1994,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/25/1044/","by":{"2":"4"}},{"t":"case","label":"Haywood v. Drown","cite":"556 U.S. 729","y":2009,"id":"2008/07-10374","sc":1,"sum":"The Court found that prohibiting civil claims against correction officers in their official capacities violated the Supremacy Clause of the Constitution.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"5, 29"}},{"t":"case","label":"Idaho v. Coeur d’Alene Tribe","cite":"521 U.S. 261","y":1997,"id":"1996/94-1474","sc":1,"sum":"Authored by Justice Anthony Kennedy, the Court ruled that the Coeur d'Alene Tribe's suit against the state officials may not proceed in federal court because States enjoy Eleventh Amendment immunity in suits filed by Indian tribes.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"41"}},{"t":"case","label":"(ctd.): INS v. Chadha","cite":"462 U.S. 919","y":1983,"id":"1981/80-1832","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Immigration and Nationality Act violated the separation of powers by allowing a one-house veto over executive decisions.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Jennings v. Rodriguez","cite":"538 U.S. 281","y":2018,"id":"2017/15-1204","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that non-citizens who are subject to mandatory detention under the Immigration and Nationality Act are not entitled to periodic bond hearings. with the possibility of release if they are detained for longer than six months.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"29"}},{"t":"case","label":"Kohl v. United States","cite":"91 U.S. 367","y":1875,"id":"scdb/1875-135","sc":1,"sum":"This was a proceeding instituted by the United States to appropriate a parcel of land in the city of Cincinnati as a site for a post-office and other, public uses.","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"26","3":"11"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lake County v. Rollins","cite":"130 U.S. 662","y":1889,"id":"scdb/1888-211","sc":1,"sum":"The.constitution of Colorado of 1876 provided that no county should contract any debt by loan in any form except for certain purposes therein named; that such indebtedness contracted in any one year should not exceed the rate therein named; and that “the aggregate amount of indebtedness of any county for all purposes... shall not at any time exceed twice the amount above herein limited,” etc. Held, that this …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Livingston v. Mayor of N.Y.","cite":"8 Wend. 85","y":1831,"court":"N.Y. Ch.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Wend./8/85/","by":{"2":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Lombardo v. Pa. Dep’t of Pub. Welfare","cite":"540 F.3d 190","y":2008,"court":"3d Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/540/190/","by":{"2":"32"}},{"t":"case","label":"McClenachan v. Curwen","cite":"6 Binn. 509","y":1802,"court":"Pa.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Binn./6/509/","by":{"2":"45"}},{"t":"case","label":"(ctd.): McDonald v. City of Chicago","cite":"561 U.S. 742","y":2010,"id":"2009/08-1521","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the Fourteenth Amendment makes the Second Amendment's right to bear arms for the purpose of self-defense applicable in the states.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"10, 27"}},{"t":"case","label":"Monell v. Dep’t of Soc. Servs. of City of N.Y.","cite":"436 U.S. 658","y":1978,"id":"1977/75-1914","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the legislative history of the Civil Rights Act of 1871, and specifically the Sherman Amendment, indicated that municipalities could be liable for the infringement of constitutional rights. Additionally, by 1871 there was a clear legislative and precedent-based history for municipal corporations — such as a school board — to be considered a “person” for the purpose of lawsuits and liability.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"36"}},{"t":"case","label":"N.Y. Trust Co. v. Eisner","cite":"256 U.S. 345","y":1921,"id":"scdb/1920-095","sc":1,"sum":"1. The Act of September 8, 1916, c. 463, Title II, § 201 et seq., 39 Stat. 777, imposes a tax on the transfer of the net estate of,every decedent, graduated according to the value as ascertained by deducting, in the ease of a resident, from the gross estate, funeral, administration and other expenses and charges,, and a specified exemption; the tax is due in one year from the decedent’s death, is payable primarily …","sumSrc":"Headnote (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"22"}},{"t":"case","label":"Patchak v. Zinke","cite":"583 U.S. 244","y":2018,"id":"2017/16-498","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that a statute that directs the federal courts to \"promptly dismiss” certain lawsuits without affecting any underlying laws does not violate the Constitution’s separation of powers principles.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"20, 44"}},{"t":"case","label":"Sosa v. Alvarez-Machain","cite":"542 U.S. 692","y":2004,"id":"2003/03-339","sc":1,"sum":"No and No. On the Alien Tort Statute claim, the Court unanimously ruled that the ATS did not create a separate ground of suit for violations of the law of nations. Instead, it was intended only to give courts jurisdiction over traditional law of nations cases - those involving ambassadors, for example, or piracy.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"44"}},{"t":"case","label":"Stroud v. McIntosh","cite":"722 F.3d 1294","y":2013,"court":"11th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/722/1294/","by":{"2":"31-32"}},{"t":"case","label":"Tarrant Reg’l Water Dist. v. Gragg","cite":"151 S.W.3d 546","y":2004,"court":"Tex.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/S.W.3d/151/546/","by":{"2":"4"}},{"t":"case","label":"(ctd.): TransUnion LLC v. Ramirez","cite":"141 S. Ct. 2190","y":2021,"id":"2020/20-297","sc":1,"sum":"Only a plaintiff concretely harmed by a defendant’s violation of the Fair Credit Reporting Act has Article III standing to seek damages against that private defendant in federal court.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"2":"14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Trant v. Oklahoma","cite":"754 F.3d 1158","y":2014,"court":"10th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/754/1158/","by":{"2":"31"}},{"t":"case","label":"Webster v. Doe","cite":"486 U.S. 592","y":1988,"id":"1987/86-1294","sc":1,"sum":"Section 102(c) of the National Security Act of 1947 (NSA) authorizes the Director of the Central Intelligence Agency (CIA), \"in his discretion,\" to terminate the employment of any CIA employee \"whenever he shall deem such termination necessary or advisable in the interests of the United States.\" After respondent, a covert electronics technician in the CIA's employ, voluntarily informed the agency that he was a …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"2":"2, 26, 42","3":"12"}},{"t":"case","label":"Ziglar v. Abbasi","cite":"582 U.S. 120","y":2017,"id":"2016/15-1358","sc":1,"sum":"The Court determined that, in suits against government officials for personally violating the Constitution, “context” should be defined narrowly for the purpose of determining whether a claim arose in a “new context,” and the government officials in this case are entitled to qualified immunity.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"2":"8, 28"}},{"t":"const","label":"Amendment V, Section 8","clauses":[["grand-jury","Grand Jury Clause"],["double-jeopardy","Double Jeopardy Clause"],["self-incrimination","Self-Incrimination Clause"],["due-process-5","Due Process Clause (Fifth Amendment)"],["takings","Takings Clause"]],"text":"No person shall be held to answer for a capital, or otherwise infamous crime, unless on a presentment or indictment of a Grand Jury, except in cases arising in the land or naval forces, or in the Militia, when in actual service in time of War or public danger; nor shall any person be subject for the same offence to be twice put in jeopardy of life or limb; nor shall be compelled in any criminal case to be a witness against himself, nor be deprived of life, liberty, or property, without due process of law; nor shall private property be taken for public use, without just compensation.","by":{"2":"18"}},{"t":"const","label":"Article I, Section 8, Clause 18","clauses":[["necessary-proper","Necessary and Proper Clause"]],"text":"To make all Laws which shall be necessary and proper for carrying into Execution the foregoing Powers, and all other Powers vested by this Constitution in the Government of the United States, or in any Department or Officer thereof.","by":{"2":"15, 21"}},{"t":"const","label":"Article I, Section 9, Clause 2","clauses":[["habeas","Suspension Clause (Habeas Corpus)"]],"text":"The Privilege of the Writ of Habeas Corpus shall not be suspended, unless when in Cases of Rebellion or Invasion the public Safety may require it.","by":{"2":"12, 41"}},{"t":"const","label":"Article I, Section 9, Clause 7","clauses":[["appropriations","Appropriations Clause"]],"text":"No Money shall be drawn from the Treasury, but in Consequence of Appropriations made by Law; and a regular Statement and Account of the Receipts and Expenditures of all public Money shall be published from time to time.","by":{"2":"18"}},{"t":"const","label":"Article III, Section 2, Clause 1","clauses":[["cases-controversies","Cases or Controversies (Justiciability)"]],"text":"The judicial Power shall extend to all Cases, in Law and Equity, arising under this Constitution, the Laws of the United States, and Treaties made, or which shall be made, under their Authority;—to all Cases affecting Ambassadors, other public Ministers and Consuls;—to all Cases of admiralty and maritime Jurisdiction;—to Controversies to which the United States shall be a Party;—to Controversies between two or more States;— between a State and Citizens of another State,—between Citizens of different States,—between Citizens of the same State claiming Lands under Grants of different States, and between a State, or the Citizens thereof, and foreign States, Citizens or Subjects.","by":{"2":"20"}},{"t":"usc","label":"5 U.S.C. § 704","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/5/704","by":{"2":"39"}},{"t":"usc","label":"28 U.S.C. § 1257","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/28/1257","by":{"2":"35"}},{"t":"case","label":"Agins v. City of Tiburon","cite":"24 Cal. 3d 266","y":1979,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Cal.%203d/24/266/","by":{"3":"2, 5, 9, 10"}},{"t":"case","label":"Agins v. City of Tiburon","cite":"447 U.S. 255","y":1980,"id":"1979/79-602","sc":1,"sum":"The zoning ordinances on their face do not take appellants' property without just compensation.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"3":"2, 3"}},{"t":"case","label":"Armstrong v. United States","cite":"364 U.S. 40","y":1960,"id":"1959/270","sc":1,"sum":"Petitioners are entitled to recover whatever value their liens had when the Government took title to the boats and materials.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"3":"14"}},{"t":"case","label":"Baker v. City of McKinney","cite":"84 F.4th 378","y":2023,"court":"5th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.4th/84/378/","by":{"3":"6"}},{"t":"case","label":"Burnham v. Super. Ct. of Cal.","cite":"495 U.S. 604","y":1990,"id":"1989/89-44","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that states have jurisdiction over nonresidents within their borders.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"3":"3, 5"}},{"t":"case","label":"Carlson v. Green","cite":"446 U.S. 14","y":1980,"id":"1979/78-1261","sc":1,"ar":1,"sum":"Respondent brought suit in Federal District Court in Indiana on behalf of her deceased son's estate, alleging that her son while a prisoner in a federal prison in Indiana suffered personal injuries from which he died because petitioner prison officials violated, inter alia, his Eighth Amendment rights by failing to give him proper medical attention. Asserting jurisdiction under 28 U. S. C. § 1331 (a), respondent …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"3":"19"}},{"t":"case","label":"DLX, Inc. v. Kentucky","cite":"381 F.3d 511","y":2004,"court":"6th Cir.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/F.3d/381/511/","by":{"3":"6"}},{"t":"case","label":"Dobbs v. Jackson Women’s Health Org.","cite":"597 U.S. 215","y":2022,"id":"2021/19-1392","sc":1,"sum":"The Constitution does not confer a right to abortion; Roe v. Wade, 410 U. S. 113, and Planned Parenthood of Southeastern Pa. v. Casey, 505 U. S. 833, are overruled; the authority to regulate abortion is returned to the people and their elected representatives.","sumSrc":"Holding (supremecourt.gov)","by":{"3":"16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Donnelly v. United States","cite":"28 Fed. Cl. 62","y":1993,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Fed.%20Cl./28/62/","by":{"3":"6"}},{"t":"case","label":"Henry v. Dubuque & Pac. R.R. Co.","cite":"10 Iowa 540","y":1860,"url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/Iowa/10/540/","by":{"3":"17"}},{"t":"case","label":"Hui v. Castaneda","cite":"559 U.S. 799","y":2010,"id":"2009/08-1529","sc":1,"sum":"The Court held that the immunity granted by the Federal Tort Claims Act precludes Bivens actions against Public Health Service officers for constitutional violations committed while in the scope of the employees' official duties.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"3":"12"}},{"t":"case","label":"Mont.-Dakota Utils. Co. v. Nw. Pub. Serv. Co.","cite":"341 U.S. 246","y":1951,"id":"scdb/1950-071","sc":1,"sum":"Petitioner and respondent are public utility electric companies engaged in interstate commerce and subject to the Federal Power Act. For ten years, while under the same management through interlocking directorates and joint officers with the approval of the Federal Power Commission, petitioner's predecessor and respondent interchanged electric energy, shared expenses and made a number of intercompany contracts …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"3":"12–13"}},{"t":"case","label":"N.Y. State Rifle & Pistol Ass’n, Inc. v. Bruen","cite":"597 U.S. 1","y":2022,"url":"https://supreme.justia.com/cases/federal/us/597/1/","sc":1,"sum":"Noting debate on when meaning is assessed.","sumSrc":"brief:3","by":{"3":"14–15, 16"}},{"t":"case","label":"Nat’l Mut. Ins. Co. of D.C. v. Tidewater Transfer Co.","cite":"337 U.S. 582","y":1949,"id":"scdb/1948-103","sc":1,"sum":"The Act is constitutional and the judgment is reversed. Pp. 583-585, 604. 165 F. 2d 531, revered. A District of Columbia corporation sued a Virginia corporation in the Federal District Court for Maryland, the jurisdiction depending solely on diversity of citizenship. The District Court dismissed the complaint. The Court of Appeals affirmed. 165 F. 2d 531. This Court granted certiorari. 333 U. S. 860. Reversed, p. …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"3":"8"}},{"t":"case","label":"Schacht v. United States","cite":"398 U.S. 58","y":1970,"id":"1969/628","sc":1,"sum":"Petitioner, who participated in a skit performed several times in front of an Armed Forces induction center demonstrating opposition to American involvement in the Vietnam conflict, was convicted by a jury of violating 18 U. S. C. § 702, which makes criminal the unauthorized wearing of an American military uniform or part thereof. Petitioner alleged that he was authorized to wear the uniform by 10 U. S. C. § 772 …","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"3":"5"}},{"t":"case","label":"Seminole Tribe of Fla. v. Florida","cite":"517 U.S. 44","y":1996,"id":"1995/94-12","sc":1,"sum":"In a 5-to-4 decision, The Court held that Congress did intend to abrogate states' sovereign immunity under the IGRA but that the Indian Commerce Clause (and by implication the Commerce Clause) did not give Congress that power. Under the Eleventh Amendment, all states are regarded as sovereign entities.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"3":"3"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. 50 Acres of Land","cite":"469 U.S. 24","y":1984,"id":"1984/83-1170","sc":1,"sum":"The Fifth Amendment does not require that the United States pay a public condemnee compensation measured by the cost of acquiring a substitute facility that the condemnee has a duty to acquire, when the market value of the condemned property is ascertainable and when there is no showing of manifest injustice.","sumSrc":"Syllabus (U.S. Reports)","by":{"3":"18"}},{"t":"case","label":"United States v. IBM","cite":"517 U.S. 843","y":1996,"id":"1995/95-591","sc":1,"sum":"Announced by Justice Clarence Thomas, the Court ruled the tax did violate the Export Clause and was therefore unconstitutional.","sumSrc":"Oyez","by":{"3":"9"}},{"t":"case","label":"Whitehead Oil Co. v. City of Lincoln","cite":"515 N.W.2d 401","y":1994,"court":"Neb.","url":"https://www.courtlistener.com/c/N.W.2d/515/401/","by":{"3":"6"}},{"t":"usc","label":"31 U.S.C. § 1304","url":"https://www.law.cornell.edu/uscode/text/31/1304","subs":["(a)"],"by":{"3":"10"}}]};